From: Matt C <latentcollapse@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 17:54:02 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/10] NIRA Stage 0 spike: NeuraBash-only session without
 kernel construction
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Finding: getting a real NeuraBash-only session, with no ipython kernel
built and no REPL_CONTROL_PROMPT doctrine, did not require touching
goals, ACP, or agent-session.ts's internals. It required forwarding an
option that already existed internally.

- AgentSession already supports baseToolsOverride, which replaces the
  entire base tool set and skips constructing IpythonKernelProvisioner
  outright (agent-session.ts _buildRuntime, existing code, unchanged).
  It just was not forwarded through the public
  createAgentSession/createAgentSessionFromServices surface. Added it
  in both places (agent-session-services.ts, sdk.ts) — ~6 lines total.
- _ensureGoalRuntimeActive already throws "Goals are disabled" when
  includeGoals is false, rather than force-reactivating ipython.
  Already correct; verified by reading, not by editing.
- REPL_CONTROL_PROMPT inclusion in the system prompt is already gated
  on hasIpython, computed from the real active tool list passed to
  _rebuildSystemPrompt on every rebuild — not a hardcoded assumption.
  Already correct; verified by reading, not by editing.
- ACP's "requires the built-in cpython tool" throw only fires when ACP
  MCP servers are actually configured, so NEURA mode's constraint is
  simply "no ACP MCP servers," not a code change.

Added packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/neurabash-preferred.ts
for the DUAL-PREFERRED arm: both tools stay active, a system-prompt
addendum reorders preference toward NeuraBash for exact/symbolic work
without claiming it is a better language than Python.

Verified with a new suite test (3/3 passing) against the existing
faux-provider harness — construction, active tool names, and doctrine
presence/absence for NEURA, DUAL, and LEGACY modes. No live model was
run. This confirms the mechanical claim only: it builds correctly. It
does not confirm model behavior under a real task, and does not rule
out goal/ACP/skill coupling that only surfaces under live end-to-end
use — that is the next gap to close before treating Route A (Prime as
native chassis) as settled rather than merely not yet falsified.

